# BACKEND · what must not enter the build context
#
# Short and hand-kept on purpose: everything in fl_backend/ that is NOT listed here reaches the
# image, because the runner copies the context wholesale. This list reads as an answer to "what does
# the image contain?". Add a line when something new here is a secret, a machine-local artifact, or
# a file nothing at run time reads.
#
# `**/` on anything that can appear at any depth: a bare pattern matches the context root ALONE, so
# `__pycache__/` would leave app/**/__pycache__ in the image. `**/` matches zero directories too, so
# the root copy is covered by the same line.

# Credentials, matched by shape rather than by inventory: each rule holds whether or not anything
# matches it right now, because a credential that reaches an image is a failure nobody sees until
# the image has shipped. The containers take their environment from compose's env_file at run time.
# The same rules are kept in every `.dockerignore` in the repository, so no build context here is
# the weaker one.
**/.env
**/.env.*
**/*.pem
**/*.key
**/*.crt
**/.npmrc

# The developer's virtualenv, built against another platform's interpreter. The image's own comes
# from the builder stage.
.venv/

**/__pycache__/
**/*.py[cod]
**/.pytest_cache/
**/.ruff_cache/
**/*.log

# `uv sync --no-dev` installs no pytest, so the suite could only ever be inert bytes in the image.
tests/

# The published document the frontend's contract test compares against (ADR-0033). The running app
# serves its OpenAPI from the live routes, so this copy is a repository artifact, not runtime input.
openapi.json

# buildkit receives both by other means; copying them in only invalidates layers.
Dockerfile
.dockerignore
